    "source_file_to_entries": {
        "raw-wiktextract-data.jsonl": [
            {
                "entry_id": "3ed7a684-1135-353a-9058-8d3aefe321f8",
                "word": "anisomerous",
                "pos": "adj",
                "lang_code": "en",
                "forms": [],
                "senses": [
                    {
                        "sense_id": "0b234e14-a03c-3a8d-a5af-3f5dbbefd706",
                        "entry_id": "3ed7a684-1135-353a-9058-8d3aefe321f8",
                        "glosses": [
                            "Having varied parts; nonuniform",
                            "Varying in number or amount of parts."
                        ],
                        "tags": [
                            "not-comparable"
                        ],
                        "examples": [
                            {
                                "example_id": "88a70492-65e1-4e67-8487-afd1eeebb9c9",
                                "sense_id": "0b234e14-a03c-3a8d-a5af-3f5dbbefd706",
                                "text": "The Elephants with hypisomerous-ridged molars are divisible into the two natural groups, Stegodon and Loxodon; the anisomerous species form a third natural group, for which, as already explained, the term Euelephas is proposed."
                            },
                            {
                                "example_id": "9ab7a4aa-16ec-41f5-b534-52bae12a3043",
                                "sense_id": "0b234e14-a03c-3a8d-a5af-3f5dbbefd706",
                                "text": "As an example, in the members of the sub-genus Euelephas, and notably the Elephas primigenius and Elephas Asiaticus, the ciphers of whose molars, he states, are precisely alike in number, he formulates their ridges in upper and lower teeth thus:—4+8+12::12+16+24, showing that, with the exception of the first and ultimate true molars, the others increase by increments of 4, or, as he terms it, by an \"anisomeroous mode of progression.\""
                            }
                        ]
                    },
                    {
                        "sense_id": "e65341aa-1abb-370e-a1b0-3fff17059d0a",
                        "entry_id": "3ed7a684-1135-353a-9058-8d3aefe321f8",
                        "glosses": [
                            "Having varied parts; nonuniform",
                            "Having the number of floral organs unequal."
                        ],
                        "tags": [
                            "not-comparable"
                        ],
                        "examples": [
                            {
                                "example_id": "cb111467-f4cf-4a9b-ba40-428e5f3c4453",
                                "sense_id": "e65341aa-1abb-370e-a1b0-3fff17059d0a",
                                "text": "A plant with four petals and six stamens is anisomerous."
                            },
                            {
                                "example_id": "a5c8e27a-d440-4ce8-b3c6-657c5c255c7b",
                                "sense_id": "e65341aa-1abb-370e-a1b0-3fff17059d0a",
                                "text": "Tamarix flowers are anisomerous ( a flower having an unequal number of petals, stamens , or other floral parts). It is possible to find in one raceme anisomerous flowers with different numbers of sepals, petals, stamens, and so on, without any numerical correlation between the organs."
                            },
                            {
                                "example_id": "989be70e-e850-4ce0-b88d-3a7fd6e8df83",
                                "sense_id": "e65341aa-1abb-370e-a1b0-3fff17059d0a",
                                "text": "Fertile stamens anisomerous or twice or thrice the number of the petals"
                            }
                        ]
                    },
                    {
                        "sense_id": "4bbc8a96-0cce-3ae1-a771-9585be60112e",
                        "entry_id": "3ed7a684-1135-353a-9058-8d3aefe321f8",
                        "glosses": [
                            "Having varied parts; nonuniform",
                            "Reflecting a nonuniform composition; indicative of differing parts."
                        ],
                        "tags": [
                            "not-comparable"
                        ],
                        "examples": [
                            {
                                "example_id": "0b6dec99-8057-48db-8d55-4086e2e18ff5",
                                "sense_id": "4bbc8a96-0cce-3ae1-a771-9585be60112e",
                                "text": "In gravel and anisomerous sand (substrate type I), we found 25 species of nematodes dominated by M. paracircumscriptus and M. itoi; the dominating trophic assemblage was \"predators\" (2B, 43.2 %)."
                            },
                            {
                                "example_id": "d9d87e53-2096-4525-a926-476fa6d26f74",
                                "sense_id": "4bbc8a96-0cce-3ae1-a771-9585be60112e",
                                "text": "In forgings where the fracture is very anisomerous (Figure 6.33 , curve 2b), the grain faces are clearly expressed."
                            }
                        ]
                    },
                    {
                        "sense_id": "0aa4a9bf-9dbd-3e8c-ae70-b1df869832e2",
                        "entry_id": "3ed7a684-1135-353a-9058-8d3aefe321f8",
                        "glosses": [
                            "Having varied parts; nonuniform",
                            "Changing in a non-uniform manner; Having different parts change by different amounts."
                        ],
                        "tags": [
                            "not-comparable"
                        ],
                        "examples": [
                            {
                                "example_id": "e935776f-9b6c-4b6c-bb15-0e31434e3544",
                                "sense_id": "0aa4a9bf-9dbd-3e8c-ae70-b1df869832e2",
                                "text": "Through these anisomerous changes the strongly benthonic body shrinks to seventy-five per cent of the length of the head shield."
                            },
                            {
                                "example_id": "183905fb-c6c4-4caa-ae37-5ca0ba283a94",
                                "sense_id": "0aa4a9bf-9dbd-3e8c-ae70-b1df869832e2",
                                "text": "In gravel and anisomerous sand (substrate type I), we found 25 species of nematodes dominated by M. paracircumscriptus and M. itoi; the dominating trophic assemblage was \"predators\" (2B, 43.2 %)."
                            }
                        ]
                    }
